# November 21, 2026: a second scatter of places across Manitoba, which had 18 (batch mb_xums and the Old Pinawa Dam), the
# thinnest state or province on the map, claimed with tools/choose.py --claim --thinnest. Sources opened in this session:
# the Manitoba Historical Society's "Historic Sites of Manitoba" page for each place (mhs.mb.ca, read with tools/page.py
# --all; each gives a site coordinate), the Province's heritage site pages (gov.mb.ca/chc/hrb), Wikipedia as raw text
# (tools/wiki_raw.py), and the pages of whoever runs each place (Parks Canada, Manitoba Parks, Public Services and
# Procurement Canada, the Friends of Upper Fort Garry, Le Musee de Saint-Boniface Museum, Daly House Museum, Sam Waller
# Museum, Stonewall Quarry Park), with two local news reports (PembinaValleyOnline 2017, CTV News 2025).
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def MHS(t,s): return ['Manitoba Historical Society: '+t,'https://mhs.mb.ca/docs/sites/'+s+'.shtml']

BATCH={'key':'mb_hlrz','label':'Manitoba: second scatter','added':'2026-11-21 07:27'}

PLACES=[
# ---- Winnipeg
N('riel-house-winnipeg','Riel House','Winnipeg (St. Vital)','mb','landmark','standing',49.81933,-97.13623,'exact','open','drive','','1880–1881',
  'A Red River frame house built in 1880-81 for Joseph Riel, brother of the Metis leader Louis Riel, and his family, who lived in it until 1969. Louis Riel never lived here, but after his execution his body lay in state in its living room for two days in December 1885. It is a National Historic Site and has been restored to how it looked in the spring of 1886.',
  'The small frame house on River Road, furnished to spring 1886, with its grounds and outdoor interpretive panels; Parks Canada suggests an hour or two. Interior open only in the summer season.',
  'The house where Louis Riel lay in state, kept as it was that spring.',
  'Riel House National Historic Site, Parks Canada, 330 River Road, St. Vital. Parking on site (Parks Canada facilities list); Winnipeg Transit buses run there from downtown. Parks Canada\'s visit page, read November 2026, said it was closed for the season until 2027 and gave the 2026 summer hours; it also lists fees, with free admission for youth.',
  [MHS('Riel House','rielhouse'),L('Parks Canada: Riel House, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/mb/riel/visit')],
  brick='no',hours='Summer only: daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., early July to late August (2026 season); closed until 2027'),
N('upper-fort-garry-gate','Upper Fort Garry Gate','Winnipeg (Downtown)','mb','fort','ruins',49.88797,-97.13541,'exact','public','walk','','1850–1882',
  'Fort Garry, the Hudson\'s Bay Company post that grew out of the North West Company\'s Fort Gibraltar of 1806, was rebuilt with stone walls in 1835; the walls were extended north in 1850, when this gateway was built. The fort was sold in 1882 and everything but the gate was pulled down. The Company gave the gate and its park to the City in 1897, and it is now Upper Fort Garry Provincial Park.',
  'The stone gateway with the Canadian Club\'s plaque of 1909, and around it a modern park laid out on the plan of the fort, where archaeologists found the west wall, a bastion and the well. A 400-foot steel Heritage Wall stands on the line of the vanished west wall and runs a sound and light show.',
  'All that is left of the fort Winnipeg grew round.',
  'Upper Fort Garry Provincial Park, 130 Main Street, open ground run with the Friends of Upper Fort Garry. No parking of its own; the nearest mapped lots are at The Forks, a few hundred metres east. The Friends\' page gives the Heritage Wall show times (weekdays 10 a.m. to 8:45 p.m., weekends from 11 a.m.), not park hours.',
  [MHS('Upper Fort Garry','upperfortgarry'),L('Friends of Upper Fort Garry','https://www.upperfortgarry.com')],
  brick='no'),
N('saint-boniface-museum-grey-nuns-convent','Grey Nuns\' Convent (Le Musée de Saint-Boniface Museum)','Winnipeg (St. Boniface)','mb','inst','standing',49.8878,-97.1231,'exact','open','walk','','1846–1851',
  'Built of oak logs in the Red River frame manner between 1846 and 1851 as the Grey Nuns\' convent, a mission house for their schooling, nursing and care of orphans and the old, it is the oldest building in Winnipeg. It has been the St. Boniface Museum since the 1960s and was renovated in the mid-1990s.',
  'The long white log building on Tache Avenue with the chapel on its rear, a Historic Sites and Monuments Board plaque in front and a City plaque for Marguerite d\'Youville. Inside are the museum\'s exhibits; original interior fabric not checked.',
  'Winnipeg\'s oldest building, put up by the Grey Nuns in the 1840s.',
  'Le Musee de Saint-Boniface Museum, 494 Tache Avenue. Its own page, read November 2026, gave the opening hours and said "estimated reopening October 19th, 2026", so check before going. Admission charged (see its page). Street parking; mapped lots along Tache Avenue.',
  [MHS('Grey Nuns Convent / Le Musee de Saint-Boniface Museum','stbonifacemuseum'),L('Le Musee de Saint-Boniface Museum','https://msbm.mb.ca'),W('Le Musée de Saint-Boniface Museum')],
  brick='no',hours='Monday to Wednesday and Saturday noon to 4 p.m., Thursday noon to 8 p.m.; closed Friday and Sunday'),
# ---- Lower Red River and the Interlake
N('st-andrews-rectory','St. Andrew\'s Rectory','St. Andrews','mb','landmark','standing',50.06609,-96.97858,'exact','public','drive','','1854',
  'Stonemason Duncan McRae built the rectory in 1854 across the road from St. Andrew\'s church; it is a fine example of mid-19th-century Red River stone building. It is a National Historic Site, and its main floor has held exhibits on Red River building and on the Church Missionary Society and the Church of England in the settlement.',
  'The two-storey stone rectory and its grounds on River Road, across from St. Andrew\'s on the Red. Parks Canada now describes a self-guided tour of the grounds of about an hour; whether the interior opens was not stated on its page.',
  'A stone parsonage of 1854 on the old Red River road.',
  'St. Andrew\'s Rectory National Historic Site, Parks Canada, 374 River Road. Free admission. Park beside St. Andrew\'s church across the road, as Parks Canada directs. Hours not given on the Parks Canada pages read November 2026.',
  [MHS('St. Andrew\'s Anglican Rectory','standrewsrectory'),L('Parks Canada: St. Andrew\'s Rectory, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/mb/standrews/visit')],
  th=[50.06656,-96.97680],thn='St. Andrew\'s church parking',brick='no'),
N('st-andrews-lock-and-dam-lockport','St. Andrews Lock and Camere Curtain Dam','Lockport','mb','canal','standing',50.08420,-96.94120,'exact','public','drive','','1908–1913',
  'Built from 1908 to a design by H. E. Vantelet of Montreal, who adapted the French "Camere" curtain dam to the Red River. The first boat locked through on May 2, 1910, and Prime Minister Laurier came for the opening that July. The steel road bridge over the dam was added in 1912-13. It is the only lock on the prairies, and a National Historic Site.',
  'The lock (62 m long, 13.7 m wide) and the dam, whose curtains, each of 50 Douglas fir laths, are taken out in sections after the navigation season, seen from the road bridge on top and from the banks; the lockhouse and a commemorative monument stand nearby. The lock still works in the navigation season and takes about 20 minutes to fill.',
  'The prairies\' only lock, with a French curtain dam unlike any other on the continent.',
  'Owned and run by Public Services and Procurement Canada. The road over the dam is open; its page (read November 2026) says the lower quay wall is closed to the public until further notice and that locking ends for the season in mid-October, after which the dam is opened. Mapped parking on River Road on the west bank.',
  [MHS('St. Andrews Camere Curtain Dam and Lock','standrewslockanddam'),L('Public Services and Procurement Canada: St. Andrews Lock and Dam','https://www.canada.ca/en/public-services-procurement/services/infrastructure-buildings/bridges-docks-dams/st-andrews-lock-dam.html')],
  th=[50.08266,-96.94254],thn='River Road parking, west bank',brick='no'),
N('stonewall-quarry-park-kilns','Stonewall Quarry Park lime kilns','Stonewall','mb','industry','ruins',50.14217,-97.32689,'site','public','walk','','about 1882–1967',
  'Limestone was quarried at Stonewall from about 1880, above all to burn into a very white quicklime prized for plaster. Brothers Enoch and Joseph Williams built a row of seven pot kilns about 1882, one for each day of the week-long burn; three taller draw kilns followed in 1904, 1906 and 1912 and ran day and night. Quarrying stopped in 1967, and the town made the quarries into a park in 1985.',
  'The row of seven pot kilns behind a chain-link fence, and the three draw kilns, the youngest taken down stone by stone and rebuilt from 2019 in the first phase of the "Save the Kilns" project. More abandoned kilns stand east across the road from the park and on Road 78N just west of Highway 7 (Historical Society page). The park also has an interpretive centre (museum) in its Heritage Arts Centre.',
  'Ten lime kilns in a row, one burn a day for a week.',
  'Stonewall Quarry Park, Town of Stonewall: open parkland with a lake, campground and walking trails, and a lot on Quarry Trail by the kilns. The interpretive centre\'s hours were not found on the park\'s page (read November 2026). The kilns outside the park are not described as public.',
  [MHS('Stonewall Limestone Quarries and Kilns','stonewallquarries'),L('Stonewall Quarry Park','https://stonewallquarrypark.ca')],
  th=[50.14125,-97.32612],thn='Quarry Park lot, Quarry Trail',brick='?'),
N('rosehill-lime-kilns','Rosehill limestone quarry kilns','Municipality of West Interlake','mb','industry','ruins',51.11097,-98.74947,'area','public','drive','','1896–1913',
  'James and Bob Young opened a quarry here in 1896, took Charles Maloan as a partner, and in 1902 formed the Manitoba Union Mining Company. Up to twelve kilns burned the local limestone into lime, which went by barge across Lake Manitoba to The Landing on the Whitemud River and on by train to Winnipeg. After two loaded barges sank in a storm the company sold its boats, and the kilns were given up, though settlers still burned lime in them now and then. The Youngs gave the property to the municipality in 1952.',
  'A pair of derelict kilns, the last of up to twelve, with a commemorative monument beside one of them.',
  'Two lime kilns left in the Interlake bush by a company that lost its barges.',
  'Municipal property (Historical Society page), with a monument for visitors; no parking is mapped and how a car reaches it was not checked. Treat old kilns as unstable.',
  [MHS('Rosehill Limestone Quarry and Kilns','rosehillquarry')],
  brick='?'),
N('hecla-historic-village','Hecla Historic Village','Hecla Island','mb','town','standing',51.13091,-96.66427,'area','public','walk','','1876–',
  'Hecla Island was settled in 1876 by the second wave of Icelandic immigrants, who called it Mikley. The island became a provincial park in 1969, and its one school closed in 1970. In the village stand the Tomasson boarding house, built in 1885 by Helgi Tomasson and Margret Thorarinsdottir to house newcomers until they had homesteads, and the Lutheran church of 1927 with its cemetery, first used in 1897.',
  'A self-guiding trail through the village: the two-storey frame Tomasson boarding house, the 1927 Lutheran church and cemetery with the centenary monument, the two-room school (now the park\'s interpretive centre), a Heritage Home furnished as an Icelandic family\'s of the 1920s to 1940s, and the Fish Station Museum. Guided tours in summer go into buildings not otherwise open.',
  'An Icelandic fishing village kept on an island in Lake Winnipeg.',
  'Hecla-Grindstone Provincial Park, Manitoba Parks (Riverton district office); off Highway 8. The village may be visited at any time; Hours from the Manitoba Parks Hecla Historic Village page, read November 2026. Mapped parking off Highway 8 beside the village.',
  [L('Manitoba Parks: Hecla Historic Village','https://www.gov.mb.ca/nrnd/parks/education-and-interpretation/hecla.html'),MHS('Tomasson Boarding House','tomassonhouse'),MHS('Hecla Island Lutheran Church and Cemetery','heclaislandcemetery'),W('Hecla-Grindstone Provincial Park')],
  th=[51.13139,-96.66354],thn='Village parking off Highway 8',brick='?',hours='Village any time; July and August: school centre Friday and Sunday 1 to 5 p.m., Fish Station Museum Saturday 1 to 5 p.m.'),
# ---- The south
N('fort-dufferin-emerson','Fort Dufferin','Emerson (Emerson-Franklin)','mb','fort','traces',49.03048,-97.20214,'site','public','drive','','1872–1879',
  'From 1872 to 1874 Fort Dufferin was the headquarters and winter quarters of the British-Canadian party of the International Boundary Commission, which marked the 49th parallel west to the Rockies. In 1874 the new North West Mounted Police gathered here before their march west, and from 1875 to 1879 more than 18,600 immigrants brought up the Red River by steamboat were housed here before moving on. It is a National Historic Site on provincial Crown land.',
  'A cairn at the entrance with three plaques, a 2015 immigrants\' monument, the restored officers\' mess kitchen, a storage shed of the fort restored as an interpretive centre (opened 2017), the hollow of the old underground stables and an 1872 latrine (Historical Society photos).',
  'Where the Mounties set out on their march west.',
  'Provincial Crown land in the Municipality of Emerson-Franklin, looked after by the Post Road Heritage Group, which restored the shed and holds a Fort Dufferin Heritage Day each year (PembinaValleyOnline, 2017). When the interpretive centre opens was not found. No mapped parking.',
  [MHS('Fort Dufferin','fortdufferin'),L('PembinaValleyOnline: New interpretive centre opens at Fort Dufferin (2017)','https://www.pembinavalleyonline.com/local/new-interpretive-centre-opens-at-fort-dufferin-3')],
  brick='?'),
N('emerson-town-hall-court-house','Emerson Town Hall and Court House','Emerson','mb','civic','standing',49.005698,-97.214050,'exact','view','drive','','1917–1918',
  'Designed by John D. Atchison and built in 1917-18, opening in July 1918, this two-storey brick and stone building was the last court house built in Manitoba\'s settlement boom of 1880 to 1920. It is a provincially designated site.',
  'The town hall on Church Street, a block east of the business street, with a Historic Sites Advisory Board sign. Inside (Historical Society photos): the courtroom, the council chamber on the second floor with the key the builder gave Mayor Ezra Casselman in 1918, and a First World War honour roll. When the public may go in not checked.',
  'A border-town court house, the last of Manitoba\'s boom years.',
  'Town hall of the Municipality of Emerson-Franklin, 104 Church Street; seen from the street. Mapped parking on 1st Street East.',
  [MHS('Emerson Town Hall and Court House','emersontownhall')],
  brick='yes'),
N('manitou-opera-house','Manitou Opera House','Manitou','mb','civic','standing',49.24089,-98.53755,'exact','view','drive','','1930',
  'Built in 1930 to replace an earlier hall burned that January, to a design by Winnipeg architect Charles S. Bridgman, unusual inside and out. It seats 385 and is a municipal heritage site (1997). In front is a bust of the suffragist Nellie McClung, who lived near Manitou from 1890 to 1911.',
  'The opera house on Main Street and the McClung bust. Inside during events only; interior fabric not checked.',
  'A small-town opera house of 1930, still booked for shows.',
  'Run as a community hall and theatre, 325 Main Street; seen from the street, inside at its events (its own site, manitouoperahouse.com, refused the page tool). Street parking.',
  [MHS('Manitou Opera House','manitouoperahouse')],
  brick='no'),
N('ninette-sanatorium','Ninette Sanatorium (Manitoba Sanatorium)','Ninette (Prairie Lakes)','mb','inst','standing',49.40317,-99.60705,'site','view','drive','','1909–1972',
  'Opened in 1909 above Pelican Lake to designs by Brandon architect W. H. Shillinglaw, the sanatorium took its first tuberculosis patient in May 1910 and grew into the province\'s largest, with more than a dozen buildings. It closed in 1972; from 1973 to 2000 it was the Pelican Lake Training Centre, and many unused buildings fell into disrepair and were pulled down. A Christian retreat used what was left, which then stood empty. CTV News reported in July 2025 that a new owner was restoring it as a museum and apartments.',
  'The former administration building of 1909 (enlarged 1918) and other remaining buildings, with monuments on the grounds: the 1960 patients\' memorial and the 1940 granite boulder for Dr. D. A. Stewart, the first superintendent.',
  'Manitoba\'s biggest TB sanatorium, empty above Pelican Lake and now being restored.',
  'Private property (sold to a new owner, CTV News, July 2025); look only from the public road. Do not enter the buildings.',
  [MHS('Ninette Sanatorium','ninettesanatorium'),L('CTV News: Ninette sanitorium to be restored by new owner (2025)','https://www.ctvnews.ca/winnipeg/article/ninette-sanitorium-to-be-restored-by-new-owner')],
  brick='?'),
N('harrison-mill-holmfield','Harrison Mill and grain elevators','Holmfield (Killarney-Turtle Mountain)','mb','industry','standing',49.13753,-99.48421,'exact','view','drive','','1897–1990s',
  'William and George Harrison came to southwestern Manitoba in 1878 and had mills at Wakopa and Killarney before settling at Holmfield, where they built a flour mill in 1897 and a large stone warehouse in 1898. The mill ran on steam, then diesel from the 1930s and electricity from 1947, and three generations of the family milled grain here until the late 1990s. It is believed to be the oldest mill in western Canada. Two 1928 Federal Grain elevators were moved beside it in the late 1940s.',
  'The mill, the stone warehouse, a coal shed and the two wooden elevators beside the old rail line. Interior not checked.',
  'Western Canada\'s oldest flour mill, with its elevators, still in one family.',
  'Private: the Harrison family still owns it (Historical Society page). Look from Railway Avenue in Holmfield.',
  [MHS('Harrison Mill and Grain Elevators','harrisonmill')],
  brick='?'),
# ---- The west
N('daly-house-museum-brandon','Daly House Museum','Brandon','mb','landmark','standing',49.8478,-99.96236,'exact','open','walk','','1882',
  'Designed by Edward McCoskrie and built in 1882 for Thomas Mayne Daly, this two-storey brick Italianate house passed in 1896 to his law partner George Coldwell, whose family lived in it until the early 1930s. From 1932 to about 1972 it was "The Maples", a children\'s shelter. A non-profit restored it and opened it as a museum in 1978.',
  'The 1882 house, the only pre-1900 building left on 18th Street, with its gardens and a Historic Sites Advisory Board plaque for Daly. The verandah went in the 1950s, when a porch, fire escape and rear wing were added. Inside are the museum\'s rooms and exhibits.',
  'Brandon\'s 1882 mansion, later an orphanage.',
  'Daly House Museum, 122 18th Street; admission and hours from its own page, read November 2026. Mapped parking beside it on Rosser Avenue and 18th Street.',
  [MHS('Daly House / Daly House Museum','dalyhousemuseum'),L('Daly House Museum','https://dalyhousemuseum.ca'),W('Daly House Museum')],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to noon and 1 to 5 p.m. in summer, 1 to 4 p.m. in winter'),
N('portage-la-prairie-old-post-office','Dominion Post Office (Portage la Prairie City Hall)','Portage la Prairie','mb','civic','standing',49.97286,-98.29010,'exact','view','walk','','1898',
  'Designed by Thomas Fuller, architect of Canada\'s first Parliament Buildings, and opened in 1898 after political wrangling in Ottawa, this Romanesque Revival building of hammer-faced limestone held the post office below and customs and revenue above. A south addition came in 1920. It became the City Hall in 1960, and over the years has also held a magistrate\'s court, an RCMP detachment with cells, the library and an arts centre.',
  'The two-and-a-half-storey limestone building with its steep mansard roof and central gable, little changed outside, and the 1983 Historic Sites and Monuments Board plaque beside it. Interior not checked.',
  'A Thomas Fuller post office of 1898 that became a city hall.',
  'Portage la Prairie City Hall, 97 Saskatchewan Avenue West; seen from the street. Mapped parking on Tupper Street North and Princess Avenue.',
  [MHS('Dominion Post Office Building / Portage City Hall','portagepublicbuilding')],
  brick='no'),
N('fort-ellice-site','Fort Ellice site','St. Lazare (Ellice-Archie)','mb','fort','marker',50.41140,-101.28783,'site','ask','drive','','1831–1890s',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company built Fort Ellice in 1831-32 near Beaver Creek, and rebuilt it here in 1862 above the Assiniboine valley, about five miles below the mouth of the Qu\'Appelle, as a trading and provisioning post on the Fort Ellice (Carlton) Trail between Fort Garry, Fort Carlton and Edmonton. A cairn unveiled on July 15, 1930 before more than 4,000 people marks the site. The Nature Conservancy of Canada bought it in 2012.',
  'A fieldstone cairn with its 1930 plaque, and a newer monument by the St. Lazare Local of the Manitoba Metis Federation to the Metis scouts, guides, hunters and interpreters of the fort, on open prairie above the valley. Nothing of the fort stands.',
  'The cairn where the trail to Edmonton left the Assiniboine.',
  'Nature Conservancy of Canada property. When it bought the site the Historical Society reported plans to make it freely open; NCC\'s visit page (read November 2026) asks visitors to contact its team to learn which properties are open. No mapped parking.',
  [MHS('Fort Ellice','fortellice'),L('DiscoverWestman: Fort Ellice 3, preserving Manitoba history through conservation (2020)','https://discoverwestman.com/local/fort-ellice-3-preserving-manitoba-history-through-conservation')],
  brick='no'),
N('church-of-the-resurrection-dauphin','Ukrainian Catholic Church of the Resurrection','Dauphin','mb','worship','standing',51.14192,-100.06202,'exact','view','walk','','1936–1939',
  'Built by parish volunteers between 1936 and 1939 under Father Philip Ruh, this stucco church with five domes is one of the cathedral-style Ukrainian churches of Manitoba, mixing Byzantine, European and Ukrainian Baroque forms. Theodore Baran painted its interior from 1957. The parish built a new church in 1990 and kept this one, which is used on special occasions; it was renovated in 2003-08 and is a provincial and national historic site.',
  'The cruciform church with its five big domes, seen across Dauphin, and inside Baran\'s icon painting (Historical Society photos of the interior). Open only on special occasions; interior visiting times not checked.',
  'Philip Ruh\'s five-domed church of the Depression years.',
  'Owned by the Ukrainian Catholic Parish of the Church of the Resurrection (Province\'s heritage page), next to its 1990 church and hall; look from the street.',
  [MHS('Ukrainian Catholic Church of the Resurrection','dauphinukrainiancatholic'),L('Manitoba Provincial Heritage Site No. 86','https://www.gov.mb.ca/chc/hrb/prov/p086.html')],
  brick='no'),
# ---- The north
N('mcardle-salt-works','McArdle Salt Works','Lake Winnipegosis (Highway 10)','mb','industry','ruins',52.86797,-101.06025,'site','view','drive','','1937',
  'In 1937 speculators from Swan River, led by Leo McArdle of Tisdale and Edward Crawford of Winnipeg, formed the Northern Salt Syndicate to boil crystal salt from a salt spring near Lake Winnipegosis. It was soon found to lose money, being far from markets and dear to ship from, and closed.',
  'Timber from the salt-making buildings and the well crib over the salt spring, beside Highway 10 (Historical Society photos of 2025).',
  'A failed northern salt works of the Depression, left by the highway.',
  'Beside Manitoba Highway 10; who owns the ground was not found, so look from the roadside. No mapped parking.',
  [MHS('McArdle Salt Works','mcardlesaltworks')],
  brick='no'),
N('sam-waller-museum-the-pas','The Pas Court House (Sam Waller Museum)','The Pas','mb','civic','standing',53.82327,-101.25263,'site','open','walk','','1916–1917',
  'When The Pas became seat of the Northern Judicial District in 1916, the town\'s first architect, George Nelson Taylor, designed this two-storey brick court house, built in 1916-17 by Snyder Brothers. Unlike other Manitoba court houses, its upper floor was a public assembly hall, the centre of the town\'s social life. The courts left in 1982 and the town made it the home of the Sam Waller Museum. It is northern Manitoba\'s oldest standing brick building.',
  'The brick court house on Fischer Avenue with the 1991 Manitoba Heritage Council plaques for the building and for the East and West Mossy Portages. Inside, the museum\'s galleries in the old courtroom and hall (Historical Society photos).',
  'The north\'s oldest brick building, now its museum.',
  'Sam Waller Museum, 306 Fischer Avenue; admission charged, by donation on Wednesdays (its hours page, read November 2026). Mapped parking just north on Fischer Avenue.',
  [MHS('The Pas Community Building and Court House / Sam Waller Museum','samwallermuseum'),L('Sam Waller Museum: hours and admission','https://www.samwallermuseum.ca/hours-and-admission-rates/'),W('Sam Waller Museum')],
  brick='?',hours='September to May: Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; June to August: Monday to Saturday 10 to 5, Sunday noon to 5'),
]

